  <h1>Privacy Notice</h1>
  <p class="lead">This notice explains what WhereITis collects, why, and what
  rights you have under the Data Privacy Act of 2012 (RA 10173). It is written to
  be read — not skimmed past.</p>

  <h2>What we collect, field by field</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="table">
      <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Where it is asked</th><th>Why we need it</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Student number</td><td>Sign-in, claims</td><td>To link a report or claim to its owner without sessions.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Full name</td><td>Sign-in</td><td>To identify you to staff when you collect an item.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Email</td><td>Sign-in</td><td>Used by the office to reach you about a claim. Not shown publicly.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Contact number</td><td>Optional, sign-in</td><td>Only if you prefer a call over an email.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Item category</td><td>Report form</td><td>To match lost and found reports of the same kind.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Item description</td><td>Report form</td><td>To help staff and other students identify the item.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Date lost or found</td><td>Report form</td><td>To score candidate matches and to compute the holding period.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Location</td><td>Report form</td><td>To narrow matches and to route the item to the right office.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Holding location</td><td>Found report form</td><td>So an owner knows where to collect the item.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>The three RA 10173 principles we apply</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Transparency</strong> — you know what is collected, why, and where
    it goes. That is what this page is for.</li>
    <li><strong>Legitimate purpose</strong> — every field above is read by at least
    one screen or report in the system. Nothing is collected "just in case".</li>
    <li><strong>Proportionality</strong> — we do not collect gender, civil status,
    religion, birthday, marital status, or any government ID number. None of those
    fields is read by any screen, so none is collected.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>How long we keep data</h2>
  <p>Reports are kept for one academic year, then archived and purged of personal
  data. The reference number and item description are retained for statistical
  purposes only. This is the retention rule implemented in the schema and in the
  custodian office's archive workflow.</p>

  <h2>Your rights under RA 10173</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Informed</strong> — read this page.</li>
    <li><strong>Access</strong> — sign in to <a href="/my-reports">My Reports</a>
    to see every report, claim, and bookmark tied to your student number.</li>
    <li><strong>Object</strong> — you may withdraw a report at any time while its
    status is <em>open</em> using the Withdraw button on the report page.</li>
    <li><strong>Erasure / blocking</strong> — email the Data Protection Officer at
    <a href="mailto:dpo@cspc.edu.ph">dpo@cspc.edu.ph</a>. The DPO will coordinate
    with the Student Affairs office.</li>
    <li><strong>Rectify</strong> — edit an open report from your My Reports page,
    or email the DPO to correct anything else.</li>
    <li><strong>Portability</strong> — email the DPO for a copy of your reports in
    a machine-readable format.</li>
    <li><strong>Damages</strong> — the RA 10173 complaints procedure applies.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Breach notification</h2>
  <p>If a personal-data breach occurs, the Data Protection Officer will notify the
  National Privacy Commission and every affected data subject within 72 hours of
  discovering the breach. The DPO makes that call; individual staff members must
  escalate any suspected breach immediately.</p>

  <h2>About this page</h2>
  <p>The first draft of this notice was written with AI assistance and then reviewed
  and edited by the project team. Every AI use is logged in the README's
  "AI-assisted development log".</p>
</main>

